# How much of each contract's roster the population reaches on its own. One row per contract
# folds/contract_subjects.sqlc counts: the subjects it declares, and the subjects some row other
# than the roster's own answers for.
#
# The other side of the principle
#   diagrams/domain_objects.sqlc states the principle for one side and this measures the other.
#   "A contract must not be derived from what it governs", and examples/compositions/main.rs
#   asserts it: every roster here is a bare VALUES literal. Nothing says the converse, and some
#   of the populations below take their subject's name from the very roster they are compared
#   against: `checks/all.sqlc` reads `r.rule`, `arithmetic/all.sqlc` reads `a.site`,
#   `algebra/all.sqlc` reads `a.law`. examples/soundness/main.rs §5 prints which, and gates it.
#
# A trade, not a defect
#   That is why it wants a number. The roster join is what buys the vacuous verdict: a rule with
#   nothing to examine still emits a row, so the verdict that matters most is a row a reader can
#   see rather than a silence. The price is that the subject is then on both sides of the
#   difference by construction. `diagrams/catalogue.sqlc` pays no such price and could not: its
#   population is `information_schema`, written by Postgres, and `diagrams/expected.sqlc` names
#   each law in its own arm as a literal.
#
# What the shortfall is and is not
#   A subject counted short is one the population reached with nothing of its own, so every
#   branch of the difference sees it only through the roster. It is not a count of failures a
#   guard would miss; read that way it imports a perturbation this relation never names. Delete
#   an arm from a population outright and branch one reports that subject whether it was short
#   here or not.
#
# SQL cannot see which populations take their key from their roster
#   It sees only where a corpus happens to make it visible. A contract built exactly like `rules`
#   shows no shortfall here while its population is complete, so a full column is not evidence
#   of a second witness. That property is a fact about the template, and
#   examples/soundness/main.rs §5 is where it lives.
#
# One row per subject, so a count of rows is a count of subjects
#   folds/contract_subjects.sqlc is keyed on (contract, subject), and every contract's subject is
#   its roster key: the rule, the site, the law slug, the domain object, the diagram law, the
#   absence or derivation column. Laws are keyed by slug and not by formula, because several laws
#   share one formula, and a count over formulas could not see one of them go missing from
#   algebra/all.sqlc.
#
# Witnessed is an answered row
#   A subject is witnessed when some row of its population answers for it: a verdict, for the three
#   contracts that carry one, and for the four that do not, the row itself. The roster's own row,
#   the one a check emits when it has nothing to examine, answers nothing, which is exactly the
#   shortfall this counts.
#
# The numbers
#   Run this relation for them; the corpus moves and a sentence does not.
#
# One fence, for the depth of the plan
#   The union is materialised, which changes no row: its plan hangs under this query's top node
#   instead of beneath the grouping, one level shallower. folds/contract_subjects.sqlc reaches
#   algebra/all.sqlc, the deepest relation in the tree, and queries/observations/16-guard-reach.sqlc
#   is read back at compile time, where a plan nested one level deeper cannot be.
-- folds/contract_subjects.sqlc counted per contract: what each roster declares, and what answers it.
WITH v AS MATERIALIZED (
    :compose(folds/contract_subjects.sqlc)
)
SELECT v.contract,
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE v.declared)                    AS subjects,
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE v.declared AND v.answered > 0) AS witnessed
FROM v
GROUP BY v.contract
